            <ArticleTitle>[Prevalence of gastro-esophageal reflux disease in patients who attended the emergency room].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>54-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is a well-known important cause of non-cardiac chest pain (NCCP). It has been shown that in western countries this condition can be in 22-66% of the cases. However, this situation is unclear in Japanese hospitals. We performed this study to clarify the frequency of GERD in NCCP patients who attended the emergency room.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We investigated the final diagnosis of the patients with chest pain who attended the emergency room of our hospital in March, June, September and December of 2005.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Forty patients with NCCP attended the emergency room in the study periods. In these patients, only one patient (2.5%) was diagnosed as GERD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of this study showed that the frequency of GERD in NCCP patients of emergency room of Japan is lower than that of western countries.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
